WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.380
When you turn your head, your inner ear reports the turn.

2
00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:09.580
Does your brain treat it like a turn someone gave you?

3
00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:11.680
Start with the receptors.

4
00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:16.880
In alert monkeys, canal afferents report active and passive turns alike.

5
00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:23.140
In freely moving mice, Pacinian touch afferents also fire strongly to the animal's

6
00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:24.140
own movements.

7
00:00:24.833 --> 00:00:27.733
So the receptors pass self-caused input to the brain.

8
00:00:28.613 --> 00:00:31.773
Whether it is damped further along, that mouse study did not test.

9
00:00:32.783 --> 00:00:33.883
Back to the monkey.

10
00:00:33.883 --> 00:00:39.083
Central vestibular-only neurons respond to head motion, not to eye movement.

11
00:00:40.067 --> 00:00:42.707
An active turn barely changes their firing.

12
00:00:43.587 --> 00:00:46.187
The same turn applied passively changes it strongly.

13
00:00:47.317 --> 00:00:50.757
Neck proprioceptors report how the head moves on the body.

14
00:00:51.617 --> 00:00:54.637
The cancellation needed them to match the command's prediction.

15
00:00:55.750 --> 00:01:00.930
Brooks and Cullen then added passive motion during an active turn, activating the

16
00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:02.150
same proprioceptors.

17
00:01:03.083 --> 00:01:08.363
Predict. With the feedback no longer matching, what do the central neurons report?

18
00:01:14.433 --> 00:01:18.033
They report the whole movement, active and passive parts together.

19
00:01:18.973 --> 00:01:20.093
The cancellation fails.

20
00:01:21.133 --> 00:01:26.033
So in these neurons, cancellation is central, and it holds only while the feedback

21
00:01:26.033 --> 00:01:27.033
matches.

22
00:01:29.617 --> 00:01:31.437
People show a related pattern.

23
00:01:32.437 --> 00:01:36.077
Self-produced touch is rated less ticklish than the same touch from outside.

24
00:01:37.167 --> 00:01:40.627
In one study, people moved a robot arm with one hand.

25
00:01:41.467 --> 00:01:43.167
A second robot touched their other palm.

26
00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:47.940
Predict. Add a delay between the movement and the touch.

27
00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:50.040
What happens to the tickle?

28
00:01:55.950 --> 00:01:57.930
It feels more ticklish with a delay.

29
00:01:58.890 --> 00:02:02.730
Rotating the hand's movement against the touch's direction does the same.

30
00:02:03.733 --> 00:02:08.253
A forward model, predicting a command's sensory result, is one candidate

31
00:02:08.253 --> 00:02:10.473
explanation, not a settled one.

32
00:02:11.467 --> 00:02:13.027
Mice show a related signal.

33
00:02:13.867 --> 00:02:18.687
Some visual cortex neurons respond when the visual flow fails to match running.

34
00:02:19.527 --> 00:02:21.247
One reading: a failed prediction.

35
00:02:22.250 --> 00:02:23.530
Back to the one idea.

36
00:02:24.470 --> 00:02:28.090
Some pathways subtract a prediction of what your own movement causes.

37
00:02:29.150 --> 00:02:30.390
Take this with you.

38
00:02:30.390 --> 00:02:35.430
In these monkey neurons, the subtraction ran only while the feedback matched the

39
00:02:35.430 --> 00:02:35.790
prediction.
